WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.199 --> 00:00:04.630
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.630 --> 00:00:07.350
This one is going to be absolutely amazing.

00:00:07.350 --> 00:00:13.599
This piece of work is aimed to help a machine
build a better understanding of images and

00:00:13.599 --> 00:00:15.059
3D geometry.

00:00:15.059 --> 00:00:20.000
Imagine that we have a large database with
these geometries and images, and we can search

00:00:20.000 --> 00:00:23.830
and compare them with arbitrary inputs and
outputs.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.660
What does this mean exactly?

00:00:25.660 --> 00:00:31.570
For instance, it can handle a text input,
such as school bus and automatically retrieve

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:37.000
3D models, sketches and images that depict
these kinds of objects.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:42.079
This is great, but we said that it supports
arbitrary inputs and outputs, which means

00:00:42.079 --> 00:00:48.380
that we can use the 3D geometry of a chair
as an input, and obtain other, similar looking

00:00:48.380 --> 00:00:50.210
chairs from the database.

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:55.600
This technique is so crazy, it can even take
a sketch as an input and provide excellent

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:57.060
quality outputs.

00:00:57.060 --> 00:01:01.860
We can even give it a heatmap of the input
and expect quite reasonable results.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:07.369
Typically, these images and 3D geometries
contain a lot of information, and to be able

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:12.880
to compare which is similar to which, we have
to compress this information into a more concise

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:13.890
description.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:16.979
This description offers a common ground for
comparisons.

00:01:16.979 --> 00:01:20.110
We like to call these embedding techniques.

00:01:20.110 --> 00:01:26.430
Here, you can see an example of a 2D visualization
of such an embedding of word classes.

00:01:26.430 --> 00:01:31.390
The retrieval from the database happens by
compressing the user-provided input and putting

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:36.829
it into this space, and fetching the results
that are the closest to it in this embedding.

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:41.200
Before the emergence of powerful learning
algorithms, these embeddings were typically

00:01:41.200 --> 00:01:42.389
done by hand.

00:01:42.389 --> 00:01:47.599
But now, we have these deep neural networks
that are able to automatically create solutions

00:01:47.599 --> 00:01:53.130
for us, that are in some sense, optimal, meaning
that according to a set of rules, it will

00:01:53.130 --> 00:01:55.689
always do better than we would by hand.

00:01:55.689 --> 00:02:00.889
We get better results by going to sleep and
leaving the computer on overnight than we

00:02:00.889 --> 00:02:05.979
would have working all night using the finest
algorithms from ten years ago.

00:02:05.979 --> 00:02:07.639
Isn't this incredible?

00:02:07.639 --> 00:02:13.260
The interesting thing is that here, we are
able to do this for several different representations:

00:02:13.260 --> 00:02:18.400
for instance, a piece of 3D geometry, or 2D
color image, or a simple word, is being embedded

00:02:18.400 --> 00:02:24.120
into the very same vector space, opening up
the possibility of doing these amazing comparisons

00:02:24.120 --> 00:02:26.860
between completely different representations.

00:02:26.860 --> 00:02:29.159
The results speak for themselves.

00:02:29.159 --> 00:02:33.740
This is another great testament to the power
of convolutional neural networks and as you

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:40.090
can see, the rate of progress in AI and machine
learning research is absolutely stunning.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:45.260
Also big thumbs up for the observant Fellow
Scholars out there who noticed the new outro

00:02:45.260 --> 00:02:48.790
music, and, some other minor changes in the
series.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:54.150
If you are among those people, you can consider
yourself a hardcore Two Minute Papers Scholar!

00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:55.150
High five!

00:02:55.150 --> 00:02:59.360
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you next time!

